  "response": "## Gate rule I'm applying\n\nNothing in this report is `high`, and the highest `agent_risk` anywhere is **0.45**. So no single finding clears a \"block on score\" bar. I'm blocking only where a finding is a **divergence trap** — where the next agent edit can silently fork behaviour or revive dead behaviour — and where the fix is `quick`/`small`. Everything else is warn (fix in a follow-up) or ignore (suppress in config, stop re-litigating it every PR).\n\nScope: 99 findings. 47 of them (`exact_duplicate_block` ×46, `duplicate_component_shape` ×1) are cross-file, not petty/single-file — handled in one lump at the bottom. The remaining 52 are the ones you asked about.\n\n---\n\n## `magic_domain_literal_scatter` — 1 finding — **BLOCK**\n\n| id | file | sev | agent_risk |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| `crime_00006` | `dashboard/final-example/app/lib/actions.ts:43` (+4 related) | medium (0.5) | **0.41** |\n\nThe literal `\"status\"` in 5 production files across both dashboard apps. This is the **highest agent_risk of any non-`sync_io` finding in the report**, and it's not an isolated one — see the two role-check findings below, which are the same defect wearing a different charge. Invoice status is a domain concept with no single definition anywhere in the codebase; every form, every action, every badge re-derives it. An agent asked to add a `\"draft\"` status has five edit sites and no way to know it missed one.\n\nBlock, but fix it as a cluster with `crime_00072`/`crime_00073` — landing a `STATUS` constant without also centralising the comparisons just moves the problem.\n\n## `duplicated_role_status_plan_check` — 2 findings — **BLOCK (same cluster)**\n\n| id | literal | sev | agent_risk |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| `crime_00072` | `\"paid\"` — 4 files, 2 expression shapes | medium (0.55) | 0.22 |\n| `crime_00073` | `\"pending\"` — 4 files, 2 expression shapes | medium (0.55) | 0.22 |\n\nOn their own these are warn-tier: `agent_risk` 0.22 is low, effort is `medium`, and the \"4 files\" is really **2 files × 2 example apps** (`edit-form.tsx` + `status.tsx`), so within a single app it's a two-site duplication, not a sprawl. But `invoice.status === \"paid\"` and `status === \"paid\"` are already two different shapes for one rule, which is exactly how the drift starts. Combined with 00006 they justify one `isPaid(invoice)` / `isPending(invoice)` policy module. Block the cluster, not the individual findings.\n\n## `commented_out_code` — 10 findings — **4 BLOCK / 6 WARN**\n\n**Block** — large disabled implementations, `agent_risk` 0.39, effort `quick`:\n\n| id | file | lines | sev |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| `crime_00008` | `starter-example/app/ui/dashboard/latest-invoices.tsx` | 19–56 (38 lines) | medium (0.45) |\n| `crime_00010` | `starter-example/app/ui/invoices/pagination.tsx` | 17–50 (34 lines) | medium (0.45) |\n| `crime_00009` | `starter-example/app/ui/dashboard/revenue-chart.tsx` | 33–62 (30 lines) | medium (0.45) |\n| `crime_00007` | `starter-example/app/query/route.ts` | 21–25 (`try`/`catch`/`await`) | medium (0.45) |\n\n0.39 is the joint-highest `agent_risk` in the dead-code group, and the mechanism is precisely the one the charge names: an agent reading `pagination.tsx` sees 34 lines of plausible implementation and no way to tell whether it's the intended behaviour or an abandoned attempt. Deleting them is `quick` and git preserves them. There is no argument for merging a file that is 60% commented-out JSX.\n\n**Warn** — small fragments, same 0.39/0.37 `agent_risk` but `low` severity (0.25) and much less to misread:\n\n- `crime_00013` / `crime_00014` — `final-` and `starter-example/app/lib/data.ts:19-20`, the commented `console.log` + `await new Promise(...)` artificial delay. Worth naming specifically: reviving these injects a deliberate multi-second stall into revenue fetching. Low severity, genuinely nasty if uncommented. Delete in the follow-up.\n- `crime_00011` (`query/route.ts:5-11`), `crime_00015` (`final-example/query/route.ts:17-20`), `crime_00012` (`revenue-chart.tsx:22-24`), `crime_00017` (`cards.tsx:21-28`, confidence 0.80 — the weakest signal in the group).\n\n## `sync_io_in_hotpath` — 6 findings — **IGNORE** (highest score in the report, and wrong)\n\n| ids | files | sev | agent_risk |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| `crime_00001`–`00005` | `basics/*/lib/posts.js`, `posts.ts` | low (0.4) | **0.45** |\n| `crime_00016` | `basics/dynamic-routes-starter/lib/posts.js` | low (0.4) | 0.37 |\n\nThese carry the highest `agent_risk` in the entire scan, and I'm ignoring all six. `getSortedPostsData` and `getAllPostIds` are called from `getStaticProps`/`getStaticPaths` — build-time only. There is no request-path event loop to block, and `getStaticPaths` can't be async-iterated into the shape the fix wants without restructuring the tutorial. The detector's \"hot path\" premise doesn't hold here.\n\nThis is the clearest case in the report of a score that should not drive the decision. If you want the scanner to stop reporting it, the durable fix is a `sync_io_in_hotpath` scope exclusion for Next.js build-time data functions, not a per-finding suppression ×6.\n\n## `direct_date` — 3 findings — **1 WARN / 2 IGNORE**\n\n**Warn** — `crime_00020`, `final-example/app/lib/actions.ts:57`, low (0.35), `agent_risk` 0.28. A genuine wall-clock read inside a server action that stamps the invoice date. Real non-determinism, real test coupling — but `agent_risk` 0.28 and `blast_radius` 0 (zero importers) means it isn't holding up a merge.\n\n**Ignore** — `crime_00018` / `crime_00019`, `*/app/lib/utils.ts:14`, `agent_risk` 0.30. Line 14 sits inside `formatDateToLocal` (L10–22, per `crime_00027`), which takes a date string as a parameter. `new Date(dateStr)` is a parse, not a clock read — deterministic, testable, correct as written. False positive; suppress by fingerprint.\n\n## `singular_plural_type_mismatch` — 2 findings — **IGNORE**\n\n`crime_00021` / `crime_00022`, `*/app/lib/utils.ts:24`, low (0.3), confidence **0.7** — the lowest confidence in the report — `agent_risk` 0.28. `revenue: Revenue[]` is correct English; revenue is a mass noun. The detector even ships its own caveat in the evidence (`v1 detector — type aliases and generic types are silently skipped`). Add `revenue` to `detectors.options.singular_plural_type_mismatch.allowedNames` and move on.\n\n## `design_token_escape` — 8 findings — **WARN (as one item)**\n\n`crime_00074`–`crime_00081`. All low (0.4), confidence 0.75, `agent_risk` **0.22**, `blast_radius` 0. Eight findings, one actual defect: `px`-valued inline styles in the invoice forms, login forms, and skeletons, mirrored across both example apps. The charge's own reasoning is the agent-copy-forward risk, and 0.22 says that risk is small. Worth a follow-up ticket to move the `18px`/`14px`/`9px`/`38px` values onto the spacing scale; not worth a merge gate.\n\n## `deep_import` — 2 findings — **IGNORE**\n\n`crime_00031` / `crime_00032`, both `react-syntax-highlighter/dist/cjs/styles/prism`, low (0.35), `agent_risk` 0.24. That deep path is the package's documented way to load a Prism theme — there is no public-entry alternative to migrate to, so the suggested action (`use_public_entry`) has nothing to point at. Allowlist the specifier.\n\n## `raster_should_be_vector` — 12 findings — **IGNORE**\n\n`crime_00082`–`crime_00093`. Low (0.35), `agent_risk` **0.16** — the second-lowest in the report — `test_gap` 0. These are 56×56 and 60×60 customer avatar photographs. \"Re-export as SVG\" is not a real instruction for a photo of a person. The detector is matching on dimensions alone and the size heuristic misfires on avatars; suppress the `public/customers/**` path rather than the twelve fingerprints.\n\n## `oversized_raster` — 6 findings — **1 WARN / 5 IGNORE**\n\n`crime_00094`–`crime_00099`. Low (0.4), confidence 0.95, `agent_risk` **0.14** — the lowest in the report, and correctly so: image weight is a page-speed problem, not an agent-correctness one.\n\n**Warn** on `crime_00095`/`crime_00096` (`hero-desktop.png`, 486.6 KB, shipped twice) — that's ~1 MB of hero image in a repo whose point is a dashboard demo, and it's a `quick` re-encode. **Ignore** the rest (237 KB OG images ×2, 422 KB `large-image.jpg` ×2 — the latter is named `large-image.jpg` in an SEO example, so it's presumably deliberate).\n\n---\n\n## Out of scope: the 47 duplication findings\n\n`exact_duplicate_block` (46) + `duplicate_component_shape` (1), all medium severity 0.55, `agent_risk` **0.22–0.25**, nearly all `blast_radius` 0. **Ignore wholesale** — and configure it, don't re-review it.\n\nEvery one of them is explained by the repo's structure rather than by a coding mistake: `dashboard/starter-example` vs `dashboard/final-example` are the same app at two tutorial checkpoints, `basics/*` is seven checkpoints of one walkthrough, and `seo/` vs `seo/demo` is the same pair again. `crime_00023` (Layout ×5), `crime_00024` (`getSortedPostsData` ×5) and the 20-odd `final`↔`starter` pairs are all checkpoint mirroring. Deduplicating them would destroy the thing the repo exists to demonstrate.\n\nThe right response is an `exclude` or a cross-package duplication scope in `crimes.config.json`, so these 47 stop consuming reviewer attention on every future PR.\n\n---\n\n## Summary\n\n**Block (2 clusters, ~6 findings):**\n1. Invoice-status policy — `crime_00006` + `crime_00072` + `crime_00073`. Highest non-false-positive `agent_risk` in the report (0.41), three charges pointing at one missing abstraction.\n2. Large commented-out implementations — `crime_00007`, `00008`, `00009`, `00010`. `agent_risk` 0.39, effort `quick`, 30–38 lines each.\n\n**Warn (13):** small dead-code fragments ×6, `direct_date` in `actions.ts`, `design_token_escape` ×8 as one ticket, `hero-desktop.png` ×2.\n\n**Ignore (~80):** all duplication (47), `sync_io` ×6, the two `formatDateToLocal` date parses, `revenue: Revenue[]` ×2, the Prism deep imports ×2, avatar rasters ×12, remaining oversized images ×4.\n\n**One caveat that changes the block list.** If these directories are genuinely tutorial checkpoints rather than a product, the four commented-out blocks in `starter-example` are the *point* — they're the \"uncomment this\" exercises students fill in — and both block items collapse to warns, leaving nothing gating the merge. That reading is well supported by the layout (`starter-example` has the dead code; `final-example` has the implementations). I've gated on the literal framing you gave me — a PR landing this on an empty baseline — but if the repo is a teaching artefact, scope `crimes` to the app you actually ship and the report drops from 99 findings to a handful.",
